/* Field schema, code lists and help text.
   HELP is the pop-up help from the Union Army program (new_mil.tcl), keyed by its old variable names.
   Edit SCHEMA to add or change fields; old records pick up new fields automatically. */
const HELP = {
"a_bclm#": "Indicates whether or not the Pension Board approved this particular disability. Disabilities approved by the Board are listed near the top of the claim form, alongside the ruling amount, as well as on the right-hand side of the middle column. Please note that, in an application for an increased disability, the Board can approve the existence of disabilities without ruling in favor of an increase.",
"a_clm#": "A disease, wound, or result for which the soldier was seeking a pension. Claims are listed in the Pension Application and at the bottom of the claim form.",
"a_sclm#": "Indicates whether or not the soldier claimed this particular disability. Disabilities officially claimed by the soldier are listed at the bottom of the claim form as well as on the left-hand side of the middle column.",
"afdesc#": "A description the pertinent information from the testimony. Use as much of the original wording as possible.",
"afdvdt#": "The date the testimony was given. MM/DD/YYYY.",
"appage#": "Age of soldier at time of pension application. This information can be found either on the pension application or the claim form. Do not infer age from any other sources.",
"appcrtn1": "Application and Certificate numbers are used to request the soldier's pension at the National Archives and are found in the General Pension Index (T-288/T-289). When the soldier applied for a pension, he was given an application number. If the pension was actually granted, he was assigned a certificate number, used by him and the pension Bureau for the duration of his pension. When the soldier died, his widow, minor children, or dependent parents could continue his pension. When they applied, they were granted new application and certificate numbers. Input these numbers from the Archives' REQUEST FOR MILITARY RECORDS form. This form is pink and attached to the file.",
"appdt#": "The date of the soldier's application. MM/DD/YYYY. The application date is found at the top of the Application/Declaration for Pension. That date is repeated at the bottom of the claim form. This field should never be left blank. If there are multiple dates to choose from, use the earliest chronological date.",
"appgrdn": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"applaw#": "The law under which the pension was granted. Use this field when the law is described by name ('GENERAL LAW') or for any amendment dates.",
"appldt#": "The law date under which the pension was granted. MM/DD/YYYY. If there are two law dates given, record both: the 'major' law (June 27, 1890, February 6, 1907, etc.) goes in this field and the amended law goes in the Law field.",
"apptyp#": "Indicates the type of pension the soldier is applying for. Until the soldier was awarded a pension, all applications are considered 'New' (N), also referred to as 'Original' on the claim form. If the application is for an increase in disabilities (more disabilities or the same problems becoming more debilitating), input as an increase Disability. If the application is submitted due to a change in the pension law itself (which allowed more money for the same disability), input the application as increase Law. The only time you select 'Re-rating' 're-Issue,' or 'Additional' is if that specific word is written at the top of the claim form.",
"arrfdt#": "Indicates the commencement date for arrears. MM/DD/YYYY. Arrears are granted to award a pension from the beginning of the wound/disease or to rectify a ruling deemed to be in error. Because the Pension Board granted arrears for a specified amount of time, arrears have both a commencement and an ending date.",
"arrtdt#": "Indicates the ending date for arrears. MM/DD/YYYY. Arrears are granted to award a pension from the beginning of the wound/disease or to rectify a ruling deemed to be in error. Because the Pension Board granted arrears for a specified amount of time, arrears have both a commencement and an ending date.",
"attrny#": "The attorney hired by the soldier to help them apply for a pension. Follows the standard name rule, with the following additions: in the case of attorney partnerships, the first name given follows the standard name rule and the rest of the information is recorded in parentheses at the end. For example, input Foxworthy and Co. as FOXWORTHY, (AND CO) and input 'John Smith and George Wesson as SMITH, JOHN (AND WESSON, GEORGE). Attorneys' names appear on both the application and claim forms.",
"batdqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the given date of battle. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"batldt01": "The date of the battle fought by soldier. MM/DD/YYYY. Battle information is found in all three Archival records. The MSR may give dates and battles for which the company was present. If a soldier was wounded in battle and treated in a hospital, battle information may be found in the CMR. In the pension file, battle information can be found in affidavits, depositions, War Department records, letters, and pension applications. Do not infer data for these fields.",
"battle01": "The place of battle fought by soldier. Battle information is found in all three Archival records. The MSR may give dates and battles for which the company was present. If a soldier was wounded in battle and treated in a hospital, battle information may be found in the CMR. In the pension file, battle information can be found in affidavits, depositions, War Department records, letters, and pension applications. Do not infer data for these fields. If you have different names for the same battle, e.g. ANTIETAM and SHARPSBURG, record the battle by the Union name (ANTIETAM). If you cannot determine if two names refer to the same battle, record both names.",
"benpens": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"c_lddt01": "Indicates the latest date at which child is known to be dead or alive. The family circular indicates whether the child is alive or dead at the time of the circular. If additional information is found later in the pension regarding whether the child is living or dead, such as a letter written by a child to the Pension Bureau, update the information to the latest date.",
"c_ldqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which child is known to be alive or dead. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"c_lord01": "Indicates whether the child is alive or dead at the date of the latest document found. The family circular indicates whether the child is alive or dead at the time of the circular. If additional information is found later in the pension regarding whether the child is living or dead, such as a letter written by a child to the Pension Bureau, update the information to the latest date.",
"c_sex01": "Child's gender. If it is not explicitly listed in a document, gender should be selected based on child's name.",
"capdtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which soldier was captured as a prisoner of war. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"captrdt1": "The date at which soldier was captured as a prisoner of war. MM/DD/YYYY. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"captrpl1": "The place at which soldier was captured as a prisoner of war. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"captrst1": "The state in which soldier was captured as a prisoner of war. Use two-digit state codes. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"cb_dqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the child's birth date. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"cb_dt01": "The date of the child's birth. MM/DD/YYYY. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's children. The next best source for children is the widow's pension application. Widow's pensions may also include childrens' birth certificates.",
"cd_dqc01": "Indicates the reliability of child's date of death. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"cd_dt01": "The date of the child's death. MM/DD/YYYY. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's children. The next best source for children is the widow's pension application.",
"chgdesc1": "Description of a change in soldier's company. A change in any of these fields is a change in the entire service; for example, if the soldier was promoted within the company, record the new rank and the company, regiment, and state, even if it is the same as the initial rank. Use the Desc. field to record service other than volunteer infantry, e.g. CAVALRY (CAV), MOUNTED INFANTRY, or NAVY, or any other necessary description. The Desc. field may also be used for explanations of the change, i.e. REDUCED TO RANKS.",
"chgdt1": "Date of soldier's change of company. MM/DD/YYYY. A change in any of these fields is a change in the entire service; for example, if the soldier was promoted within the company, record the new rank and the company, regiment, and state, even if it is the same as the initial rank.",
"chgqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the given date for soldier's change of company. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"chname01": "Child's name. The standard name rule applies; do not repeat the soldier's surname, but do record married daughters' last names. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's children. The next best source for children is the widow's pension application. Comments may include STEP, ADOPTED, and FOSTER. Unnamed children should be input as '(CHILD)'. Unnamed deceased children should be input as '(DEAD CHILD)'.",
"compsl_01": "Compensation for Service of Slave",
"condadjs": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"condsaty": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"condtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which soldier was confined as a prisoner of war. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"confndt1": "The date at which soldier was confined as a prisoner of war. MM/DD/YYYY. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"confnpl1": "The place at which soldier was confined as a prisoner of war. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"confnst1": "The state in which soldier was confined as a prisoner of war. Use two-digit state codes. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"dchdt#": "The soldier's date of discharge. MM/DD/YYYY. This information can be found in the MSR (indicated on the Muster-Out rolls) and in the pension.",
"dchhd#": "Indicates whether soldier was honorably or dishonorably discharged. Only honorably discharged soldiers were eligible for a pension. This information can be found in the MSR and in the pension.",
"dchqc#": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which soldier was discharged. If a soldier is mustered out with his company (indicated on the Company Muster-Out Roll in the MSR), input a Quality Code of 7. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"dchrn#": "The reason for the soldier's discharge. This information can be found in the MSR and in the pension.",
"dponet#": "Indicates the person giving testimony in the affidavit or deposition. We are not interested in the name of the person giving testimony, only their relationship to the soldier.",
"enlst_fs": "If you know whether the soldier was a slave or a free man at the time of his enlistment, record that information here.",
"fb_date": "The date of soldier's father's birth. MM/DD/YYYY. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit. The frequency for finding birth and/or death information on parents is low, but is very important to record to aid in the census collection.",
"fb_dtqc": "Indicates the reliability of father's date of birth. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"fd_date": "The date of soldier's father's death. MM/DD/YYYY. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit. The frequency for finding birth and/or death information on parents is low, but is very important to record to aid in the census collection.",
"fd_dtqc": "Indicates the reliability of father's date of death. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"fthrname": "Soldier's father's name. The standard name rule applies; do not repeat the soldier's surname. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit.",
"homecond": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"illwnd#": "Indicates whether the problem is an illness (I) or a wound (W)",
"ilwdsc#": "Description of the wound or illness. Include all descriptors, i.e. ACUTE DIARRHEA, CHRONIC RHEUMATISM, GSW L LEG, etc. If you enter data into the Illness/Wound field, you must enter the description of the illness or wound, even if you just type SICK or W.",
"ilwsev#": "Description of the severity of the illness or would. Examples include ACUTE, CHRONIC, FATAL, SEVERE, SLIGHT, FLESH. Note that often severity descriptors, such as ACUTE or CHRONIC, are entered in both the Description field as well as the Severity field.",
"info1t#": "Indicates a general idea of what the given testimony is about.",
"info2t#": "Indicates a general idea of what the given testimony is about.",
"infodt#": "The specific date, if given, of the information in the affidavit. MM/DD/YYYY.",
"infotm#": "Indicates the general time period for the information in the affidavit.",
"lopsdys": "The number of days given for length of pensionable service. The length of pensionable service is found only on the pension ruling form in the pension file after 1907. Record this information exactly as it appears on the pension ruling form; do not infer or convert days to months.",
"lopsmth": "The number of months given for length of pensionable service. The length of pensionable service is found only on the pension ruling form in the pension file after 1907. Record this information exactly as it appears on the pension ruling form; do not infer or convert days to months.",
"lopsyrs": "The number of years given for length of pensionable service. The length of pensionable service is found only on the pension ruling form in the pension file after 1907. Record this information exactly as it appears on the pension ruling form; do not infer or convert days to months.",
"lsdqc#": "Indicates the reliability of the date of soldier's enlistment. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"lsdys#": "The number of days for which the soldier was enlisted. The standard periods of enlistment during the Civil War were 90 days, one year, or three years. This information can be found on the enlistment papers, either in the MSR or War Department papers. Record this information as it is found, i.e. do not convert 90 days to 3 months.",
"lsmth#": "The number of months for which the soldier was enlisted. The standard periods of enlistment during the Civil War were 90 days, one year, or three years. This information can be found on the enlistment papers, either in the MSR or War Department papers. Record this information as it is found, i.e. do not convert 90 days to 3 months.",
"lstag#": "Soldier's age at enlistment. The enlistment papers or the muster-in card from the MSR almost always gives age at enlistment. Do not infer age at enlistment from other sources.",
"lstdt#": "The date of the soldier's enlistment. MM/DD/YYYY. Most soldiers have a specific muster-in card in the MSR that gives information about the soldier's enlistment. The date and place of enlistment can also be found in the pension record, both in oral testimony and War Department papers.",
"lsyrs#": "The number of years for which the soldier was enlisted. The standard periods of enlistment during the Civil War were 90 days, one year, or three years. This information can be found on the enlistment papers, either in the MSR or War Department papers. Record this information as it is found, i.e. do not convert 90 days to 3 months.",
"mb_date": "The date of soldier's mother's birth. MM/DD/YYYY. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit. The frequency for finding birth and/or death information on parents is low, but is very important to record to aid in the census collection.",
"mb_dtqc": "Indicates the reliability of mother's date of birth. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"md_date": "The date of soldier's mother's death. MM/DD/YYYY. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit. The frequency for finding birth and/or death information on parents is low, but is very important to record to aid in the census collection.",
"md_dtqc": "Quality code for md_date",
"mentcond": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"milcact1": "Indicates the nature of the military action being described. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milcfdt1": "The earliest date at which the military action applied, i.e. the beginning of a furlough or the date of desertion. MM/DD/YYYY. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milcfqc1": "The earliest date at which the military action applied, i.e. the beginning of a furlough or the date of desertion. MM/DD/YYYY. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milcleg1": "The action taken by the Army against the soldier, if arrested. Examples include CHARGES DROPPED, FINED, IN CONFINEMENT, CONFINED TO HARD LABOR, etc. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milcplc1": "The city, battle, camp, etc. where the military action occurred. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milcrsn1": "The reason given for a soldier's arrest or desertion. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milctdt1": "The latest date at which the military action applied, i.e. a return from furlough or desertion. MM/DD/YYYY. The MSR is the main source for information about military action. However, the pension file is used to corroborate and often expands on information from the MSR because it determined a soldier's qualification for a pension.",
"milctqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the latest date at which soldier underwent the described military action. Complete dates which are not specifically marked as beginning or end dates should be marked with a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"mildfdt1": "The earliest date at which soldier was assigned to his duty. MM/DD/YYYY. Information about duties is most often found in the MSR. If you come across another document with the same duty with an earlier date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, earlier, date.",
"mildfqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the earliest date at which soldier was assigned to his duty. Complete dates which are not specific dates of assignment or release from a duty should be marked with a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"mildtdt1": "The latest date at which soldier was assigned to his duty. MM/DD/YYYY. Information about duties is most often found in the MSR. If you come across another document with the same duty with a later date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, later, date.",
"mildtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the latest date at which soldier was assigned to his duty. Complete dates which are not specific dates of assignment or release from a duty should be marked with a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"milduty1": "Specialized assignment or service performed by the soldier; examples include COMPANY COOK, MUSICIAN, TEAMSTER, or DETACHED SERVICE. Standard abbreviations for this variable are CO COOK, MUS, TMS, and DET SER. Information about duties is most often found in the MSR. For naval service, record the name of the ship the sailor served on, i.e. USS MERRIMACK, in the Duties field and the dates of his service on that ship in the From and To fields.",
"mthrname": "Soldier's mother's name. The standard name rule applies; do not repeat the soldier's surname. Indicate mother's maiden name in parenthesis: '(nee xxxx)'. Parental information is found in the pension record; it can be found in affidavits, depositions, and letters. If the pension file type is a dependent parent, this information is always present. On rare occasions, a parent's name is found in the Military Service Record, especially in the case of a minor recruit.",
"ownnm_01": "If there is information on the soldier's former owners, record it here. You can find information on the owner in the MSR of USCT soldiers and in their pension files. There is a More? to collect information on more than one owner.",
"pardtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which soldier was paroled as a prisoner of war. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"paroldt1": "The date at which soldier was paroled as a prisoner of war. MM/DD/YYYY. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"parolpl1": "The place at which soldier was paroled as a prisoner of war. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"parolst1": "The state in which soldier was paroled as a prisoner of war. Use two-digit state codes. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"penftype": "The pension file's final classification. If only the soldier applied for or received a pension, record the file type as \"I\" (invalid). If the soldier's widow continues the pension, or receives her own, record the file type as \"W\" (widow). \"P\" designates a pension received by dependent parents and \"M\" a pension received by a minor child or a dependent major child. This information is apparent from the request form that comes with the pension.",
"physcond": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"powrslt1": "Description of results regarding the soldier's status as a prisoner of war, including transfers to other prisons, information about death, escape, furlough, etc. Prisoner of war information is most often found on the POW form in the MSR; input information exactly as it appears on that slip. Information may also be found on War Department records in the pension file. Do not infer information.",
"pr_fdqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the earliest known date at which parents lived at this residence. Complete dates for residences which are not exact move-in or move-out dates will usually have a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.Indicates the reliability of the earliest known date at which parents lived at this residence. Complete dates for residences which are not exact move-in or move-out dates will usually have a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"pr_fmdt1": "The earliest known date at which parents lived at this residence. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same residence with an earlier date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, earlier, date.",
"pr_fmpt1": "Indicates for which parent this residence information applies. Parents' residences can be found on pension applications, affidavits, depositions, letters, and envelopes. Parents' residential information is extremely useful in searching the earlier censuses when the recruit may still be living at home.",
"pr_tdqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the latest known date at which parents lived at this residence. Complete dates for residences which are not exact move-in or move-out dates will usually have a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"pr_todt1": "The latest known date at which parents lived at this residence. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same residence with a later date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, later, date.",
"r_fdqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the earliest known date at which soldier lived at this residence. Complete dates for residences which are not exact move-in or move-out dates will usually have a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code. The correct use of quality codes is crucial for making residential information comprehensible.",
"r_fmdt01": "The earliest known date at which soldier lived at this residence. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same residence with an earlier date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, earlier, date. Residential information is found throughout the pension record on many different forms, including all documents the soldier submitted to the Pension Bureau. While collecting a pension record, you must constantly update residence information. The correct use of quality codes is crucial for making this information comprehensible.",
"r_lvwdt1": "The date at which soldier is living with someone. MM/DD/YYYY. This information can be found in letters and affidavits.",
"r_lvwth1": "The name of any person with whom the soldier is living. It is assumed that the soldier lives with his parents until he marries and with his spouse until she dies; if the soldier is living with anyone else, record that information here. This information can be found in letters and affidavits.",
"r_lwdqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date at which the soldier is living with someone. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"r_lwrln1": "The relation between the soldier and the person with whom he is living. Examples include SISTER, FRIEND, LANDLADY, EMPLOYER, etc.",
"r_str01": "The street address at which soldier lived at a given time. Residential information is found throughout the pension record on many different forms, including all documents the soldier submitted to the Pension Bureau. While collecting a pension record, you must constantly update residence information. The correct use of quality codes is crucial for making this information comprehensible.",
"r_tdqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the latest known date at which soldier lived at this residence. Complete dates for residences which are not exact move-in or move-out dates will usually have a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code. The correct use of quality codes is crucial for making residential information comprehensible.",
"r_todt01": "The latest known date at which soldier lived at this residence. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same residence with a later date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, later, date. Residential information is found throughout the pension record on many different forms, including all documents the soldier submitted to the Pension Bureau. While collecting a pension record, you must constantly update residence information. The correct use of quality codes is crucial for making this information comprehensible.",
"ra_cplqc": "Indicates the reliability of soldier's given complexion.",
"ra_cplxn": "Soldier's complexion. Use two-digit appearance codes. Appearance information appears in the Military Service record and enlistment papers.",
"ra_eyec": "Soldier's eye color. Use two-digit appearance codes. Appearance information appears in the Military Service record and enlistment papers.",
"ra_eyeqc": "Indicates the reliability of soldier's given eye color.",
"ra_hairc": "Soldier's hair color. Use two-digit appearance codes. Appearance information appears in the Military Service record and enlistment papers.",
"ra_harqc": "Indicates the reliability of soldier's given hair color.",
"rb_dtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the soldier's given death date. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rd_bdtqc": "Quality code for rd_bgdt.",
"rd_bgdt": "The beginning date of the cause of soldier's death. MM/DD/YYYY. Death certificates often include beginning dates for soldier's cause of death and contributing cause of death.",
"rd_cause": "The cause of soldier's death. Death certificates and military records (if soldier died in service) often contain cause of death. Separate multiple causes with slash marks (/).",
"rd_cbdqc": "Indicates the reliability of this piece of information. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rd_cbgdt": "The beginning date of the contributing cause of soldier's death. MM/DD/YYYY. Death certificates often include beginning dates for soldier's cause of death and contributing cause of death.",
"rd_ccaus": "The contributing cause to soldier's death. Death certificates can often include a specifically-labelled contributing cause; only when it is clearly marked on such a document should causes be listed here. Separate multiple causes with slash marks (/).",
"rd_date": "Date of soldier's death. MM/DD/YYYY. If no death date is found in the pension file, add a remark with latest document date ('Death: No Date Given Last Document Dated mm/dd/yyyy')",
"rd_dtqc": "Indicates the reliability of the soldier's date of death. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rd_illdy": "The length, in days, of the soldier's terminal illness. Death certificates often include length of death illness. Input length exactly as seen on the certificate.",
"rd_illmn": "The length, in years, of the soldier's terminal illness. Death certificates often include length of death illness. Input length exactly as seen on the certificate.",
"rd_illyr": "The length, in years, of the soldier's terminal illness. Death certificates often include length of death illness. Input length exactly as seen on the certificate.",
"rd_stype": "Indicates the most complete source of information about soldier's death. Death certificates include the date and place of death, the cause and contributing cause, and the length of the illness. Other sources are less complete. Pensions granted after 1915 have a much better chance of containing death certificates. Separate multiple causes with slash marks (/). Specify 'Other' source in remarks ('Death: Source xxxx'). If no death date can be found, add a remark with latest document date ('Death: No Date Given Last Document Dated xx/xx/xxxx')",
"reason#": "An explanation for a ruling in the case of a rejection or decrease. If the ruling is a rejection or a decrease, there will be an explanation for the change. The most common responses in this field are SOLDIER DROPPED or ADMITTED SUBJECT TO THE ELECTION OF THE CLAIMANT ALREADY ON ROLLS AT EQUAL OR HIGHER RATE UNDER OTHER LAW. If the reason is simply NO INCREASE, leave this field blank.",
"recname1": "Soldier's name. Name rule ('LAST NAME, FIRST NAME MIDDLE NAME'). Name information is found throughout the documents. All variant spellings must be collected in order to aid the census collection.",
"recomdtn": "In the late 1920's and 1930's, the Veterans Administration conducted a home survey of elderly veteran pensioners. They were interested in the health and welfare of the pensioners. One of the questions asked the examiner to report on the mental condition of the pensioner. Input the answer to the question exactly as it appears on the document.",
"recslave": "Record whether the soldier is a former slave. This information may be found in the MSR or pension record.",
"religon1": "Soldier and spouse's religion. Religion information is usually found on marriage or baptismal records.",
"rf_stdt1": "Soldier's state of finances. A soldier's state of finances is found in affidavits, depositions, pension applications, and letters. It is usually represented by key descriptive words such as DESTITUTE, GREATLY IN NEEED, or DEPENDENT ON CHARITY OF OTHERS. This field should always be filled out, even if the response is UNKNOWN (with no date).",
"rf_stof1": "Soldier's state of finances. A soldier's state of finances is found in affidavits, depositions, pension applications, and letters. It is usually represented by key descriptive words such as DESTITUTE, GREATLY IN NEEED, or DEPENDENT ON CHARITY OF OTHERS. This field should always be filled out, even if the response is UNKNOWN (with no date).",
"rf_stqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the information given about a soldier's state of finances. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rh_date1": "The date at which soldier's height was recorded. MM/DD/YYYY. Height is usually found in Military Service enlistment papers and war department records. It can also be found in affidavits and depositions.",
"rh_feet1": "Soldier's height. Note that feet and inches are recorded in separate fields; convert all fractions to decimals. Height information is extremely important to this study. The date of each height change is equally important. Height is usually found in Military Service enlistment papers and war department records. It can also be found in affidavits and depositions.",
"rh_fiqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the soldier's height information. Only label a height as a discrepancy (5) if a soldier is reported to have two different heights on the same date.",
"rh_inch1": "Soldier's height. Note that feet and inches are recorded in separate fields; convert all fractions to decimals. Height information is extremely important to this study. The date of each height change is equally important. Height is usually found in Military Service enlistment papers and war department records. It can also be found in affidavits and depositions.",
"ri_edtqc": "Indicates the reliability of the soldier's date of entry. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"ri_entdt": "The date of soldier's immigration to the United States. MM/DD/YYYY. Death certificates often include immigration information.",
"ri_numyr": "Number of years that an immigrant soldier has lived in the United States. Death certificates often include immigration information.",
"rl_pds01": "A description of the soldier's ability to do labor at a given time, if labor force is not given as a percentage or fraction. Labor force participation information is found most frequently in affidavits. It is often a subjective opinion of neighbors, co-workers, employers, friends, or family about the amount of work the soldier could do.",
"rl_pdt01": "The date at which this labor force information applies to the soldier. MM/DD/YYYY. The date can either be the date of the document from which information is found, or a date specified by an affidavit (i.e. 'Soldier was 100% disabled as of 1876'). If multiple dates are given, create a window of time by inputting each date in a separate field.",
"rl_pqc01": "The reliability of the date associated with this labor force information. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rl_prt01": "A percentage rating of the soldier's ability to do labor at a given time. Convert fractions to percentages, i.e. 1/4 is 25%, 2/3 is 67%, 1/2 is 50% and 'wholly disabled' is 100%. Labor force participation information is found most frequently in affidavits. It is often a subjective opinion of neighbors, co-workers, employers, friends, or family about the amount of work the soldier could do.",
"rl_wd01": "Indicates whether the percentage refers to the amount of work the soldier can do (W) or the extent to which he is disabled (D).",
"rlgnqc1": "Indicates the reliability of information about the soldier and spouse's religion.",
"rlgnsrc1": "The most complete source of marriage data. This field is intended to collect information on the source of the marriage data and should be updated as you come across better sources. It is important that this field reflects the best and most complete source document.",
"ro_fdt01": "The earliest known date at which soldier was employed at this occupation. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same occupation with an earlier date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, earlier, date.",
"ro_fqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the earliest known date at which soldier was employed at this occupation. Dates which are not exact hiring or firing dates should be input with a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"ro_job01": "Soldier's occupation. Occupational information is found in enlistment papers of the Military Service Record, in declarations for pension, and in testimonial papers of the pension record.",
"ro_tdt01": "The latest known date at which soldier was employed at this occupation. MM/DD/YYYY. If you come across another document with the same occupation with a later date, delete the date already input in this field and replace it with the new, later, date.",
"ro_tqc01": "Indicates the reliability of the latest known date at which soldier was employed at this occupation. Dates which are not exact hiring or firing dates should be input with a Quality Code of 9. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"rulamt#": "The monthly dollar amount of the ruling given to the soldier. Convert all fractions to decimals. The Pension Board's ruling appears on the claim form, along with the pension commencement date, which usually corresponds to the Application Date.",
"rulfdt#": "The commencement date of the ruling. MM/DD/YYYY. The Pension Board's ruling appears on the claim form, along with the pension commencement date, which usually corresponds to the Application Date.",
"rulidr#": "Indicates whether the given ruling is an increase from the previous ruling, a decrease from the previous ruling, or a rejection. If the pension type is New, leave this field blank.",
"rultdt#": "The ending date of the ruling. MM/DD/YYYY. An ending date is given on the claim form in rare instances, particularly if the pensioned cause is expected to improve or the soldier dies during the application process.",
"rw_amt01": "The dollar amount of this wealth and income information, indicated by the document. Information about the soldier's estate is often found in widows' papers, as well as other documents in the pension. Affidavits and depositions sometimes contain economic data such as wages or income. Anything dated within a year of the soldier's death is considered his wealth/income unless specifically stated otherwise.",
"rw_des01": "Description of the type of wealth and income information. Information about the soldier's estate is often found in widows' papers, as well as other documents in the pension. Affidavits and depositions sometimes contain economic data such as wages or income. Anything dated within a year of the soldier's death is considered his wealth/income unless specifically stated otherwise.",
"rw_ind01": "The date of the document from which this wealth and income information was taken. MM/DD/YYYY. Information about the soldier's estate is often found in widows' papers, as well as other documents in the pension. Affidavits and depositions sometimes contain economic data such as wages or income. Anything dated within a year of the soldier's death is considered his wealth/income unless specifically stated otherwise.",
"rw_inf01": "Indicates the type of wealth and income information being described. Information about the soldier's estate is often found in widows' papers, as well as other documents in the pension. Affidavits and depositions sometimes contain economic data such as wages or income. Anything dated within a year of the soldier's death is considered his wealth/income unless specifically stated otherwise.",
"rw_ins01": "The source of the wealth and income information being described. Information about the soldier's estate is often found in widows' papers, as well as other documents in the pension. Affidavits and depositions sometimes contain economic data such as wages or income. Anything dated within a year of the soldier's death is considered his wealth/income unless specifically stated otherwise.",
"rw_iqc01": "The reliability of the date associated with the wealth and income information. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"sb_date1": "The date of sibling's birth. MM/DD/YYYY. Sibling information is found in affidavits, depositions, letters, Bible records, and census forms. Sibling information is also found if a sibling provides testimony in an affidavit or deposition. Birthdates can be inferred from the document's date and the age of the sibling at the time of the document.",
"sb_dtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date of sibling's birth. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"sc10rmks": "Remarks for Screen 10. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to. The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears. If there is any information on the Veterans Administration form that you are not able to fit in the screen variables input it here.",
"sc1rmks": "Remarks for Screen 1. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to (i.e. 'DEATH: xxxx'). The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears.",
"sc2rmks": "Remarks for Screen 2. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to (i.e. 'DEATH: xxxx'). The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears.",
"sc3rmks": "Remarks for Screen 3. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to (i.e. 'DEATH: xxxx'). The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears.",
"sc5rmks": "Remarks for Screen 5. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to (i.e. 'DEATH: xxxx'). The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears.",
"sc9rmks": "Remarks for Screen 9. All information collected in a Remarks field must be labeled according to the entry field it relates to (i.e. 'DEATH: xxxx'). The information in each Remarks field is specific to the screen on which it appears.",
"sibname1": "Sibling's name. The standard name rule applies; do not repeat the soldier's surname, but do include sisters' married names. Sibling information is found in affidavits, depositions, letters, Bible records, and census forms. Sibling information is also found if a sibling provides testimony in an affidavit or deposition. Record STEP, HALF, ADOPTED, etc. or any title in parentheses. If the name of the sibling is not recorded, but there is an indication that there were siblings, record this information in parentheses: '(BROTHER)' or '(SISTER)'.",
"timefrdt": "If you know when the slave was freed input it here.",
"unftc#": "The reason why the soldier is unfit for duty. Take this information directly from the Certificate of Disability for Discharge, found in the MSR and pension. Do not infer this information.",
"unftd#": "The number of days the soldier was unfit for duty before his discharge. Take this information directly from the Certificate of Disability for Discharge, found in the MSR and pension. Do not infer this information.",
"vaformdt": "Veterans Administration form date.",
"vafrmage": "Veteran's age from the Veterans Administration form.",
"wb_date1": "The date of wife's birth. MM/DD/YYYY. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's wife or wives. Spouse information is also found in affidavits, depositions, pension application forms, marriage certificates, death certificates, and letters.",
"wb_dtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date of wife's birth. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"wd_date1": "The date of wife's death. MM/DD/YYYY. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's wife or wives. Spouse information is also found in affidavits, depositions, pension application forms, marriage certificates, death certificates, and letters.",
"wd_dtqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the date of wife's death. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"wifname1": "Wife's name. Spouse names are input in the following format: MARRIED LAST NAME, FIRST NAME MIDDLE INITIAL (NEE MAIDEN NAME; COMMENTS). For example, Jacqueline Kennedy Onassis would be input as ONASSIS, JACQUELINE (NEE BOUVIER; JACKIE). Do not repeat the soldier's surname. The family circular is the best source of information about the soldier's wife or wives. Spouse information is also found in affidavits, depositions, pension application forms, marriage certificates, death certificates, and letters.",
"wm_date1": "The date of soldier's marriage to wife. MM/DD/YYYY. Marriage certificates are the best source for marriage information. The next-best source is the family circular. Widow's pension applications will also include marriage information.",
"wm_dors1": "Indicates whether the soldier and wife were divorced or separated. Information about divorces and separations will be found in the family circular and affidavits. If a separation is followed by a divorce, record the divorce information here and the separation information in the Remarks field.",
"wm_dsdt1": "Indicates the date of any divorce or separation between soldier and wife. MM/DD/YYYY. Information about divorces and separations will be found in the family circular and affidavits.",
"wm_dsqc1": "Indicates the reliability of the information input about soldier's divorce or separation from wife.",
"wm_dtqc1": "The reliability of the date of soldier's marriage to wife. If you input an incomplete date (i.e. with dashes in it), you must record a Quality Code.",
"wm_ofct1": "The officiator of soldier and wife's marriage. The standard name rule applies; comments include the title or position of the officiator, such as Minister of the Gospel '(MG)', Reverend '(REV)', Priest '(PRIEST)', or Justice of the Peace '(JP)'. Marriage certificates are the best source for marriage information. The next-best source is the family circular. Widow's pension applications will also include marriage information.",
"wm_plac1": "The place of soldier and wife's marriage. The marriage place includes such things as BRIDE'S HOME, METHODIST CHURCH, etc.",
"writes#": "Indicates whether or not the soldier writes. Y/N. There is often a statement on the bottom of the claim form indicating whether the claimant writes (Claimant does _____ write'). If there is a statement to the effect that the claimant signs by mark, record N in this field. If it is ambiguous, leave this field blank."
};
